-----------------------------
- Object Based Programming - 
-----------------------------

You have already been doing some object based programming. Object based programming is basically procedural programming 
with objects. Here is an example.

****
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Driver {
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		Scanner input;
		input = new Scanner(System.in);
		
		System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
		String name = input.nextLine();

		System.out.println("Hello, " + name + "!" );
	}
}
****

We know that Java's import statement is used to bring some functionality from the language's standard library 
(or another library) into our program. In this case we want to use the Scanner class located in Java's util package. 
A Scanner object can be used to read and interpret input from the console keyboard, disk files, and other sources.

We also remember that Java requires you to declare your variables before you can use them, and the declaration includes 
the data type. So, on line 05 we are declaring a variable named input of type Scanner.

Scanner is a class, and classes are like cookie cutters or like blueprints. We use classes to help us create objects of 
some specific type. On line 06 above we are creating a new Scanner object and assigning that object to the variable 
named input. Creating an object is called instantiation. On line 06 we are instantiating a Scanner object.
